{% partial file="./_breaking-changes-caution.mdoc" /%}

## Quick Overview

- Modules live in `/modules`
- Product type modules should start with "Service" (e.g., `Servicehosting`)
- Module IDs must be lowercase
- Modules can add: API endpoints, templates, product types, cron jobs, hooks, and more

## Directory Structure

Module names start with a capital letter:

- ✅ **Valid:** `Example`, `Mynewmodule`, `Servicehosting`
- ❌ **Invalid:** `example`, `MyNewModule`, `servicehosting`

See the [file structure docs](/extensions-and-development/file-structure/#srcmodules) for the complete layout.

## Example Module

The best way to learn is by example. Our [example module](https://github.com/FOSSBilling/example-module) demonstrates:

- Admin and client area templates
- Email templates
- API routes
- Using the dependency injection container
- Routes with variables (`/example/user/:id`)
- Translations
- Interacting with the database
- Event hooks

For template-specific helpers, see [Twig Filters & Functions](/extensions-and-development/twig-filters/). For browser-side API forms and links, see the [JavaScript API Wrapper](/extensions-and-development/javascript/).

## Running Scheduled Tasks

Use `onBeforeAdminCronRun` or `onAfterAdminCronRun` for scheduled work. Define the handler in `Service.php` and adapt the event-specific method name and body in the [Event Hooks example](/extensions-and-development/event-hooks/#using-hooks-in-your-module).

## Module Permissions

Define custom permissions for staff members in your module.

### Setting Up Permissions

```php
public function getModulePermissions(): array
{
    return [
        'delete_something' => [
            'type' => 'bool',
            'display_name' => __trans('Delete something'),
            'description' => __trans('Allows the staff member to delete "something"'),
        ],
        'can_always_access' => true,
        'manage_settings' => [],
    ];
}
```

`can_always_access` grants baseline access to the module. Declaring `manage_settings` registers the standard boolean permission for editing the module's settings.

### Checking Permissions

In PHP handlers, use `checkPermissionsAndThrowException()` to enforce a permission for the current staff member:

```php
$this->di['mod_service']('Staff')->checkPermissionsAndThrowException('example', 'delete_something');
```

In admin Twig templates, use `has_permission()` to show controls for that permission:

```twig {% process=false %}
{% if has_permission('example', 'delete_something') %}
    <button type="button">{{ 'Delete something'|trans }}</button>
{% endif %}
```

Pass just the module ID, as in `has_permission('example')`, to check module access. Enforce permissions in the PHP handler even if Twig hides the control.

## Getting Started

{% steps %}
1. Copy the [example module](https://github.com/FOSSBilling/example-module)
2. Rename files and update the manifest
3. Start building your functionality
4. Test in a development environment
{% /steps %}

## The Manifest

The `manifest.json` file describes your module. The canonical specification is [`manifest.schema.json`](https://github.com/FOSSBilling/modules/blob/main/manifest.schema.json) in the [FOSSBilling/modules](https://github.com/FOSSBilling/modules) repository, which CI validates every published module against.

Required fields:

| Field | Description |
|---|---|
| `manifest_version` | Specification version — use `1` |
| `type` | Must be `"module"` |
| `id` | Lowercase module identifier; must match the directory name (case-insensitive) |
| `name` | Display name shown in the admin panel |
| `version` | Semantic version (no `v` prefix) |
| `description` | One-line description |
| `author` | Object with `name` and optional `url` |
| `license` | SPDX license identifier (e.g. `Apache-2.0`) |
| `requires` | Compatibility requirements (`fossbilling`, `php`, `modules`) |

Optional fields: `homepage_url` (an http(s) URL).

### Icons

Module icons are shipped as a file in the module folder rather than declared in the manifest: `icon.svg`, `icon.png`, `icon.webp` or `icon.avif` (at most one; SVG preferred). FOSSBilling picks it up automatically — no manifest entry is needed.
